# HecRecruit — Clean MVC Architecture

Keep the architecture strictly clean and simple. Never mix responsibilities between layers.

## Package responsibilities

| Layer | Package | Responsibility only |
|-------|---------|---------------------|
| Model | `com.IHEC.Recruit.model` | JPA entities, enums, composite keys — data structure only |
| Repository | `com.IHEC.Recruit.repository` | Spring Data JPA — database access only |
| Service | `com.IHEC.Recruit.service` | Business logic, transactions, orchestration |
| Controller | `com.IHEC.Recruit.controller` | HTTP mapping, session checks, redirect/flash, populate `Model`, return view names |
| View | `src/main/resources/templates/` + `static/css/` | Thymeleaf display — no business rules |

## Rules by layer

### Models
- No HTTP, session, or UI concerns
- No service or repository calls
- Validation annotations on entities are OK; complex validation belongs in services

### Repositories
- Extend `JpaRepository` (or similar); custom queries only
- No business rules, no DTO shaping for views

### Services
- Inject repositories (and other services), never controllers
- Throw domain-appropriate exceptions; let controllers map to redirects/messages
- Reuse existing services instead of duplicating logic

### Controllers
- Inject **services only** — never repositories
- Thin handlers: read request/session → call service → set model attributes → return view or redirect
- Session → user resolution may live in a private helper or `SessionControllerAdvice` for cross-cutting model attributes
- No SQL, no multi-step business workflows inline

### Views (Thymeleaf)
- `th:text`, `th:each`, `th:if`, `th:href`, `th:action` for display and links
- Shared layout/CSS via `hec-ui.css` and fragments
- JavaScript only when Thymeleaf cannot suffice (confirm dialogs, small UX) — no SPA frameworks
- No business calculations or database logic in HTML

## When changing code

1. Place new logic in the correct layer first; refactor if a file already violates separation
2. Prefer extending existing `*Service` / `*Controller` over new parallel classes
3. Keep diffs minimal — no over-engineering, no duplicate helpers
4. Match existing naming (French routes/messages, `resoudreCandidat`-style helpers)

## Examples

```java
// BAD — business logic in controller
@PostMapping("/postuler")
public String postuler(@RequestParam int offreId, HttpSession session) {
    Offre o = offreRepository.findById(offreId).get();
    if (candidatureRepository.existsBy...) { ... }
}

// GOOD
@PostMapping("/postuler")
public String postuler(@RequestParam int offreId, HttpSession session, RedirectAttributes ra) {
    Candidat c = resoudreCandidat(session);
    if (c == null) return "redirect:/login";
    candidatureService.postuler(c.getCin(), offreId);
    return "redirect:/candidat/dashboard";
}
```

```html
<!-- BAD — scoring in template -->
<span th:text="${offre.salaire * 0.1 + candidat.score}"></span>

<!-- GOOD — computed in service, passed as model attribute -->
<span th:text="${scoreAffiche}"></span>
```
